# 9. TECHNICAL IMPLEMENTATION REQUIREMENTS

Before coding, inspect the existing stack and architecture.

Use the current framework and build system wherever possible.

### Frontend
- Reuse existing React and TypeScript conventions.
- Maintain clear component boundaries.
- Prefer reusable hooks and components.
- Avoid unnecessary global state.
- Keep types accurate.
- Handle loading, errors, and empty results.
- Ensure routes and navigation remain functional.

### Styling
- Establish reusable design tokens.
- Avoid duplicated CSS.
- Preserve existing Tailwind conventions if present.
- Scope styles to avoid unintended regressions.
- Implement a consistent glassmorphism system.
- Respect reduced-motion preferences.

### Backend
- Preserve existing server routes and middleware.
- Add endpoints only where needed.
- Validate API responses.
- Use appropriate timeouts and error handling.
- Avoid blocking the application when a third-party service is unavailable.
- Cache data when appropriate.
- Keep secrets on the server.
- Distinguish API errors from valid empty results.

### Scientific data
Prefer authoritative sources, including:
- NASA APIs.
- NASA Exoplanet Archive.
- NASA/JPL Small-Body Database and related services.
- NASA SPHEREx mission resources.
- NASA image and astronomy resources.
- Other appropriate scientific catalogues.

Inspect existing integrations before introducing new ones.

Do not assume that every NASA resource exposes the same API format or has unrestricted access.

Document required credentials, API limitations, and configuration.

### Reliability
- Preserve valid existing routes.
- Avoid broken imports.
- Avoid unnecessary dependency changes.
- Verify JSON import handling.
- Verify TypeScript compatibility.
- Avoid browser-only APIs during server-side rendering unless properly guarded.
- Ensure new components do not crash if optional data is missing.

### Accessibility
- Support keyboard navigation.
- Provide visible focus states.
- Use semantic HTML.
- Give meaningful controls accessible names.
- Maintain adequate contrast.
- Avoid communicating critical status through color alone.
- Respect reduced-motion settings.

